rd ratio convinced me to stick with a standard minimum raise to stay safe.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: You are holding King of Clubs and Queen of Hearts (KQo), which is a premium starting hand in a heads-up match (top 15% of hands). You are the Small Blind, which acts as the Button in heads-up play, giving you the advantage of acting last post-flop. You are currently trailing in the cumulative chip count, so you need to accumulate chips. Folding is incorrect due to hand strength. Calling (limping) is passive and allows the opponent to see a free flop with weak hands that could crack yours. Raising is the optimal play to build the pot for value, assert initiative, and utilize your position.\n\nReason: KQo dominates the vast majority of hands the opponent will hold. By raising, you force the opponent to pay to see the flop, potentially taking down the pot immediately or building value when you are likely ahead. A raise to 200 (the minimum raise) is a standard, low-risk opening that keeps the opponent's range wide while establishing aggression.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
